A||||13||TRL46202||Bogbone question||||5||
Z||000000||TRL46202||12-15-1999||04:21 PM||||What do I have to do to replace the uper dogbone on my 87GT. Do I have to put anything under the engine. Also what other mounts should I look at to see if they need replaced, has anyone done this without removal of the engine and where are they located....<P>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||12-15-1999||05:32 PM||||TRL, The dog bone is a easy job, better with two people, one can remove it. My son and I did it in about 20min. Once the bone is bolted to the engine you need to pry/pull the engine to trunk, one person to pry and one to line up the other bolt and pop it in.<BR>As for the other mounts I have not done them but you can read "Torque Strut" in this fourm or try the search opition.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||12-15-1999||05:38 PM||||Dogbone is one of the easiest things to replace, Had my 17 year old daughter replace the one on the 87SE she drives. Remove the black plastic triangle, right beside the right shock tower, that will give you access to the other end of the bolt. I had to put a pry bar in the upper dogbone mounting and pull or push a little so she could get the bolt through, but very easy. The rest of the mounts, you will have to put a jack under your oil pan (don't bend it) but they aren't bad either.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>NOTE:<BR>87 and 88 dogbones are different form previous years. They look the same and are the same length but 87, 88 have a taper inside where the rubber bushing is. You can put an earlier one on your car. GOOD LUCK!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000003||JetroGT||12-16-1999||05:14 AM||||If you don't have a pry bar just have one side of the dog bone bolted on then have a friend rock the car back and forth while it is in park. This will move the engine enough where you can slide the other bolt through.||||reg||3
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||06:22 AM||||Watch your fingers if you do it this way.||||reg||1
Z||000005||pherder||12-16-1999||07:33 PM||||FYI, RCC sells a replacement V6 dogbone for $29.95 the last time I looked.<P>It isn't any thing special but it does the job.  <P>Poly bushings, approx. 1.5" diameter.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||12-16-1999||07:49 PM||||I'm surprised no one's asked what a "<B>b</B>ogbone" is yet.. hehe..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||SpeedPhreak||12-16-1999||08:31 PM||||whats a dog bone?||||reg||6
Z||000008||Eric||12-16-1999||08:46 PM||||I know what a dogbone is, but what is a bogbone?||||reg||5
Z||000009||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||02:42 AM||||It's a bone found in a bog, duh! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||dogtired||12-17-1999||10:07 AM||||What is a dogbone?<BR>Well I think we are getting a little personal here, but Ill take a shot at it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>This is one of the areas where the 4cyl car may feel a little better equipped than the 6cyl cars cause theirs is about 3 inches longer than ours.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>But they both do the same job. They have rubber on each end (some folks purchase an aid to insert in these ends, such as a real hard plastic, some have even talked about wood, but watch for splinters) and are there to minimize vibration and movement. Caution make sure your nuts are tight or it will flop around, and can cause damage to other vital organs. So take care of your dog bone, if you try and tell your spouse you can't preform because of an inadequate dogbone they get very upset. ;0||||reg||6
Z||000011||dogtired||12-17-1999||10:45 AM||||WELL    thought I should take a shot at that definition before penetrator had his way with it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
